# L §4.3: "as if {wording D for [x, y]} keeps every law date and restricts the named items' candidates ... to versions
# that entered into force by D (enters, §5.3)".  L §5.3: "With no enters, it is the later of known and the start of
# governs."  The replacing scale row (v2, governs 2026.., known 2026-06-15) entered into force on 2026-06-15:
# in the wording of 2026-06-30 it is read (2); in the wording of 2026-06-01 only v1's row (1).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

input scale: int

def y: int at reader
  rule base = scale

version v1
  cite s
  governs 2025..
  known 2025-01-01
  scale = 1

version v2
  cite s
  governs 2026..
  known 2026-06-15
  replace scale = 2

ask q1: y law 2026-08-01
ask q2: y as if {law 2026-08-01, wording 2026-06-30 for [scale]}
ask q3: y as if {law 2026-08-01, wording 2026-06-01 for [scale]}
=== expect
q1: 2
  law date 2026-08-01, cut 2026-12-31
q2: 2
  pinned: law 2026-08-01
  pinned: wording 2026-06-30 for [scale]
  cut 2026-12-31
q3: 1
  pinned: law 2026-08-01
  pinned: wording 2026-06-01 for [scale]
  cut 2026-12-31
